## 1. Win/loss post-mortem methods (the "determine why" branch)

| Technique | What it does | When to use | Trade-offs | Recommendation here |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Structured loss taxonomy + checklist | Classify each loss: price too high / scope mismatch / spec non-compliance / missing doc / competitor advantage / client history | Immediately; every closed tender | Cheap but partly subjective; needs consistent categories to be aggregatable | **Do first.** A fixed `loss_reason` enum in the feedback record (schema below) makes later stats possible. Human picks category, AI drafts the narrative |
| Delta-vs-winner decomposition | Compare our bid vs winning price per position group; "we are 10% expensive vs X on dimmers" | When competition application data (todo's "link to tender information") is retrievable | Requires competitor line prices - often only total is public; position-level inference is estimation | **Core of the post-mortem UI.** Store both observed totals and inferred per-group deltas, marked `observed` vs `estimated` |
| Feature attribution (SHAP) on win model | Attribute win probability to price, margin, competitor count, client, scope size | Only once a tabular win-model with 50+ outcomes exists | Needs `shap` (heavy, non-stdlib); unstable with correlated features; slow on small data | **Defer.** With <50 tenders use plain logistic regression coefficients + likelihood-ratio tests (stdlib-implementable); add SHAP only if/when a real model trains on hundreds of records |
| Classic bidding models (Friedman / Gates) | Win-probability-vs-markup curves fitted from historical bid ratios (our bid / lowest competitor) | Once ~20-30 bid-ratio observations exist | Assumes stationary competition & independence; per-competitor fits need even more data | **Good fit for _3_Prediction**, fed by the records _4 accumulates. Fit per-client-type, not per-competitor, until data volume justifies it |
| Competitor benchmarking table | Running stats per competitor: median gap %, win count, tenders seen | Continuous, from every outcome record | Swiss procurement publishes winner + sometimes all bids per canton - retrieval effort is the cost | **Implement as a periodic recompute over the record store** (like `step4` summaries), not a live query |
| Paired before/after review | Compare what changed between our last N winning vs losing bids (margin, factors, scope) | After each model/version switch | Confounds time-based drift | **Cheap and interpretable; include in the generated report** |

## 2. Bayesian updating for small-sample win rates (the "update estimation of success" branch)

| Technique | What it does | When to use | Trade-offs | Recommendation here |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Beta-Binomial conjugate updating | `win_rate ~ Beta(alpha0 + wins, beta0 + losses)`; prior carries between tenders | Any sample size; this is the correct small-n tool | Choice of prior matters at n<5; point estimate hides uncertainty | **Primary technique.** ~15 lines of stdlib (`math`, no scipy needed for mean/CI via closed forms or a few thousand `random.betavariate` draws). Store `(alpha, beta)` per segment, update on each outcome |
| Empirical-Bayes shrinkage | Segment rates (per client type / competitor / region) shrunk toward the global rate proportional to segment size | When some segments have 1-3 observations | Slightly more code; needs all records present | Use the simple version: `shrunk = (alpha0 + w) / (alpha0 + beta0 + n)` with `alpha0+beta0 ~= 10` pseudo-counts; document the constant |
| Thompson sampling | Sample from each variant's Beta, pick the action with the best sample | For choosing among candidate margins/variants when outcomes are sparse and sequential | Off-policy bias if selection is not logged; exploration costs money in tenders | **For factor/margin exploration only, conservative epsilon.** Log which variant *would* have been chosen so offline eval stays honest |
| Hierarchical Bayes / partial pooling | Full multi-level model across segments | n > 100 per level | Requires PyMC/Stan - violates the stdlib-lean constraint | Skip for now; the shrinkage heuristic above captures 80% of the value |
| Price-elasticity from sparse outcomes | Logistic regression of win on log(price ratio): coefficient = elasticity proxy | After ~30 outcomes with price variation | Observational data is confounded (we bid high on hard jobs); no true counterfactual | Fit the 1-2 parameter logistic model in stdlib (grid search / Newton on log-likelihood); report the CI, treat point estimate skeptically; the "10% more expensive than competitor X" observation is exactly this feature |

## 3. Continuous learning for LoRA adapters (feedback -> updated model, "allow fallback/switch")

| Technique | What it does | When to use | Trade-offs | Recommendation here |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Full-data incremental fine-tune (retrain from base on all accumulated data) | Each new dated version = base model + entire accumulated dataset | Default for small datasets (< few thousand examples) | Compute cost grows; slow drift of behavior | **Default here.** Data volume is small (tenders are monthly-ish); retraining on base+cumulative data is the simplest anti-forgetting method |
| Replay buffer / rehearsal mix | Mix a fixed fraction of old examples into each incremental update | When incremental training from the previous adapter | Extra bookkeeping (the buffer IS the uploads store); selection policy matters | The `uploads/lora_data` files already form a natural replay corpus - keep them forever, always include prior data in any incremental job |
| Regularization (EWC / L2-SP / weight averaging) | Penalize drift from previous weights to protect old skills | Long task sequences | Tuning-sensitive; EWC needs Fisher computation | Overkill at this scale; note for later |
| Adapter merging / task vectors | Merge or arithmetic-combine LoRA deltas | Combining weak datasets; unlearning outliers | Merge artifacts (rank/alpha mismatch, task interference) | Not needed while each task trains separately (which the per-task version lists already model) |
| Catastrophic-forgetting guard via golden set | After training, re-run a fixed eval suite; reject if regression on old capabilities exceeds threshold | Always, before any promotion | Needs a curated eval set that stays valid | **Key recommendation - mirror `validate.py`.** One small golden set per task (kept in git, like `materials/expected_output_example.json`), scored on every new version |
| Shadow / champion-challenger evaluation | New version runs in parallel on live inputs; outputs logged, not served; compare metrics over a window | Before "selected" flips - the fallback/switch decision | Doubles inference cost during window; needs outcome patience | **The gating mechanism (next section).** "Shadow" fits this repo: score new adapter on the same tenders, human confirms promotion |
| A/B (split traffic) | Randomly serve old/new to real traffic | High-volume settings | Tender volume is far too low and each bid too consequential for live A/B | **Reject** - shadow + golden-set eval instead |

