### R2.4 Deepened: Beta-Binomial / EB shrinkage - formulas + computed small-n examples

Formulas verified against Wikipedia (Conjugate prior: Beta-binomial posterior = `Beta(alpha0 + wins, beta0 + losses)`, expressed there as `alpha + Sigma x_i, beta + Sigma(N_i - x_i)`; Empirical Bayes method: Beta-binomial is the canonical parametric EB model; Robbins' nonparametric `(y+1) P(y+1)/P(y)` noted but needs many exchangeable units - not our case).

```
posterior mean (shrunk rate): E[p|w,n] = (alpha0 + w) / (alpha0 + beta0 + n)
95% credible interval:        seeded quantiles of random.betavariate(a, b)  (stdlib)
MoM prior from segment rates m, v:  common = m(1-m)/v - 1;  alpha0 = m*common;  beta0 = (1-m)*common
```

Computed today with stdlib only (prior `Beta(3,7)`, i.e. pseudo-count total 10 around global mean 0.30):

| data | raw rate | shrunk mean | 95% CrI (Beta) | Wilson 95% CI (no prior) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 win / 1 | 1.000 | **0.364** | [0.122, 0.652] | [0.207, 1.000] |
| 3 wins / 3 | 1.000 | **0.462** | [0.211, 0.723] | [0.438, 1.000] |
| 0 wins / 1 | 0.000 | **0.273** | [0.067, 0.555] | [0.000, 0.793] |
| 2 wins / 8 | 0.250 | **0.278** | [0.103, 0.500] | [0.071, 0.591] |
| 5 wins / 5 | 1.000 | **0.533** | [0.289, 0.769] | [0.566, 1.000] |

Read: shrinkage converts "we always win school HVAC" (3/3) into "46% +- a huge interval" - exactly the anti-overfit message round 1 wanted, now with numbers. The Beta CrI is also *narrower* than Wilson at the extremes because the prior excludes 0/1 rates.

**Method-of-moments trap (new finding):** on 6 toy segments (9 wins/30 tenders overall), MoM over between-segment rate variance yields `alpha0+beta0 = 0.7` - a nearly vacuous prior that barely shrinks 1/1 (0.701, still absurd). With few segments the MoM variance estimate is itself noisy and *over*estimates heterogeneity. **Practical rule: floor the pseudo-counts at `alpha0+beta0 >= 5` (prefer ~10) and cap at ~30; document the constant in `settings.yaml`, revisit quarterly.** This supersedes round 1's "alpha0+beta0 ~= 10, document the constant" - it is now a floor, not just a default.

### R2.5 Deepened: evaluation-gate / promotion design (what threshold over how many samples)

- **The ML Test Score** (Google, research.google.com/pubs/the-ml-test-score... - abstract fetched): 28 concrete tests across data / model / infrastructure / monitoring. Our gate is the "model" slice: feature sanity, golden-set eval, score-stability over time. Useful as the checklist that the `eval` block must cover before `promote` is offered in the UI.
- **Hamel Husain, "Your AI Product Needs Evals"** (hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/ - fetched): evaluation ladder = Level 1 scoped unit tests (golden set) -> Level 2 human+model eval on logged traces (humans must keep sampling traces; track human-vs-auto correlation) -> Level 3 A/B. Maps 1:1 onto our design: golden suite (L1), human confirm on promote screen (L2), A/B rejected for volume (round 1) - consistent.
- **The honest statistics (the round-2 computation that matters):** proving *superiority* of a challenger win-model needs sample sizes in the hundreds (detecting a 5pp win-rate change at 80% power ~ 4n/(delta)^2 order - hundreds of monthly tenders = decades). Therefore the gate must be **non-inferiority** (clinical-trial concept: challenger acceptable if its CI on the primary metric does not extend worse than a pre-set margin delta beyond the champion), plus human judgment. Concretely converged:
  - `input_processing` / `productivity_factors`: golden-set gate only - `mean_score_new >= mean_score_active - 0.02` over >= 50 curated examples (score = per-example 0..1, e.g. normalized match quality / exact-match on expected extraction), AND no single golden example below a hard floor (0.5). With n=50, a 2pp margin is resolvable; with n<30, require strictly >= champion.
  - `win_ratio`: golden gate + outcome backtest - Brier score of challenger vs active on all recorded decided tenders, **challenger Brier <= active Brier + 0.02 with n >= 10 decided tenders** (out-of-time: train data must predate the eval window; Brier is the proper scoring rule for probabilistic outputs, Wikipedia "Scoring rule"/"Brier score" fetched 200).
  - Both gates produce a written `eval` record (below) so a human can confirm promotion with evidence, never auto-flip `selected`.

